rail.prompt:
You are a code reviewer tasked with identifying "Long Method" type technical debts in the provided code snippets.
Please review the code snippet and identify any instances of Long Method technical debt.

Below, you will find a definition of "Long Method" technical debt along with example code snippets.
Each example pair consists of a "Bad Example" that includes "Long Method" technical debt and a "Good Example" where the technical debt has been resolved.

######################################################
Technical Debt: Long Method  - It refers to method in software that is overly long.
    A method that contains too many lines of code, violates principles like Single Responsibility and leading to code that's hard to understand, maintain, and test. 
    Such methods often encapsulate multiple functionalities, making them prone to bugs and difficult to modify. 
    Simplifying these methods or breaking down into smaller, more cohesive units can enhance code readability and maintainability.

    Bad Example 1.1: Long Method

public class UserManager {
    public void processUser(int userId) {
        // Validate user ID
        if (userId <= 0) {
            System.out.println("Invalid user ID provided.");
            return;
        }

        // Load user data
        String userData = loadUserData(userId);
        if (userData == null) {
            System.out.println("No user found with ID: " + userId);
            return;
        }

        // Process user orders
        List<Order> orders = getUserOrders(userId);
        if (orders.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("User has no orders.");
            return;
        }

        for (Order order : orders) {
            if (order.isDelivered()) {
                System.out.println("Order " + order.getId() + " already delivered.");
            } else {
                // Assume processOrder is a method that processes the order
                processOrder(order);
                System.out.println("Processed order: " + order.getId());
            }
        }

        // Update user activity log
        updateUserActivityLog(userId, "Processed orders.");
    }

    private String loadUserData(int userId) {
        // Assume this method returns user data as String or null if not found
        return "UserData";
    }

    private List<Order> getUserOrders(int userId) {
        // Assume this method returns a list of user orders
        return new ArrayList<>();
    }

    private void processOrder(Order order) {
        // Assume this processes the order
    }

    private void updateUserActivityLog(int userId, String activity) {
        // Log user activity
    }
}

        - Issue: The method handles multiple tasks such as user validation, loading user data, and processing user orders all in one. This makes it long and complex which makes it hard to maintain.

    Good Example 1.2: Short method

public class UserManager {
    public void processUser(int userId) {
        if (!validateUserId(userId)) {
            return;
        }

        String userData = loadUserData(userId);
        if (userData == null) {
            System.out.println("No user found with ID: " + userId);
            return;
        }

        processUserOrders(userId);
        updateUserActivityLog(userId, "Processed orders.");
    }

    private boolean validateUserId(int userId) {
        if (userId <= 0) {
            System.out.println("Invalid user ID provided.");
            return false;
        }
        return true;
    }

    private String loadUserData(int userId) {
        // Assume this method returns user data or null if not found
        return "UserData";
    }

    private void processUserOrders(int userId) {
        List<Order> orders = getUserOrders(userId);
        if (orders.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("User has no orders.");
            return;
        }

        for (Order order : orders) {
            processOrder(order);
        }
    }

    private List<Order> getUserOrders(int userId) {
        // Assume this method returns a list of user orders
        return new ArrayList<>();
    }

    private void processOrder(Order order) {
        if (order.isDelivered()) {
            System.out.println("Order " + order.getId() + " already delivered.");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Processed order: " + order.getId());
            // Assume this processes the order
        }
    }

    private void updateUserActivityLog(int userId, String activity) {
        // Log user activity
    }
}

        - Benefit: The long method is broken down into smaller methods, each handling a specific part of the process. This improves readability and makes the code easier to maintain.

    Bad Example 1.3: Long Method

public class CustomerService {
    public void setupNewCustomer(String email, String name, String address) {
        if (email == null || email.isEmpty() || !email.contains("@")) {
            System.out.println("Invalid email address.");
            return;
        }

        if (name == null || name.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("Invalid name.");
            return;
        }

        if (address == null || address.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("Invalid address.");
            return;
        }

        int customerId = createNewCustomerAccount(email, name, address);
        if (customerId <= 0) {
            System.out.println("Failed to create customer account.");
            return;
        }

        sendWelcomeEmail(email);
        System.out.println("New customer account created successfully with ID: " + customerId);
    }

    private int createNewCustomerAccount(String email, String name, String address) {
        // Simulate account creation logic
        return (int) (Math.random() * 1000);
    }

    private void sendWelcomeEmail(String email) {
        // Simulate sending an email
        System.out.println("Welcome email sent to: " + email);
    }
}

        - Issue: The method handles setting up a new customer account, including data validation, account creation, and sending a welcome email, all in one overly long and complex method.

    Good Example 1.4: Short Method

public class CustomerService {
    public void setupNewCustomer(String email, String name, String address) {
        if (!isValidCustomerData(email, name, address)) {
            return;
        }

        int customerId = createNewCustomerAccount(email, name, address);
        if (customerId <= 0) {
            System.out.println("Failed to create customer account.");
            return;
        }

        sendWelcomeEmail(email);
        System.out.println("New customer account created successfully with ID: " + customerId);
    }

    private boolean isValidCustomerData(String email, String name, String address) {
        if (email == null || email.isEmpty() || !email.contains("@")) {
            System.out.println("Invalid email address.");
            return false;
        }
        if (name == null || name.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("Invalid name.");
            return false;
        }
        if (address == null || address.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("Invalid address.");
            return false;
        }
        return true;
    }

    private int createNewCustomerAccount(String email, String name, String address) {
        // Simulate account creation logic
        return (int) (Math.random() * 1000);
    }

    private void sendWelcomeEmail(String email) {
        // Simulate sending an email
        System.out.println("Welcome email sent to: " + email);
    }
}


        - Benefit: The large method is broken down into smaller methods, improving readability and maintainability. 
        Each method now handles a specific task, enhancing modularity and ease of testing.

